═══ R8 EN reply complete (collagen/vegetarian) — guard: clean ═══
Q: I have spondylosis and osteoporosis and my doctor requires me to take collagen as part of treatment. But I have been a vegetarian for 14 years. Can I take normal collagen?
Sources: Metaphysics Q&A Vol 3 (EN) | Buddhism Q&A (EN) | Metaphysics Q&A Vol 3 (EN) · types: qa_metaphysics,qa_general,introduction
  ✓ substantial reply (>600 chars)
  ✓ ends cleanly (punctuation/emoji, not mid-word)
  ✓ answers the vegetarian-collagen question
  ✓ final text passes guard (0 residual)
--- reply ---
Fourteen years of vegetarian practice is not a small thing — I understand why this question troubles you 🙏

Master Lu addressed this exact situation. In *Buddhism Q&A*, he was asked about supplements containing animal ingredients:

> "When you consume any medicine or health supplements that contain ingredients such as gelatine or fish oil, if your body does not reject the taste, then perhaps you have not reached the level where you are completely observing your precepts. However, it would still be best to avoid consuming such products as they contain animal by-products."

So Master Lu's guidance is: **avoid animal-based collagen if there is any way around it.** Most collagen supplements are made from fish skin/scales or bovine/porcine hide, so they are animal by-products.

**Practical steps I'd suggest:**

1. **Go back to your doctor and ask specifically for a vegetarian option.** Tell them clearly you have kept a vegetarian vow for 14 years. There are plant-based/vegan "collagen builder" formulations, and doctors often prescribe collagen simply as a source of amino acids, calcium and joint support — which can frequently be met another way. This is a medical decision, so please let your doctor decide the substitute, not me.

2. **Support your bones through vegetarian nutrition**, which Master Lu encouraged:

> "We should ensure that we have a balanced diet, as we need to take care of our bodies. Buddhas and Bodhisattvas would also like us to have a healthy body so that we can practise Buddhism better."

He specifically recommended soy products (soybeans, tofu, tofu sheets, soy milk) for protein, and milk and dairy for calcium — all acceptable for vegetarians.

3. **If, after all that, your doctor says there is genuinely no alternative** and your health is at real risk — Master Lu taught that intention is what matters most, and that in a no-choice situation you should speak to the Bodhisattva in your heart:

> "Under extreme circumstances when you have no choice... you should say to Guan Yin Bodhisattva in your mind that you are consuming these vegetables because you have no choice, and you do not want to eat them at all."

> "Most importantly, it is all about your intention."

Taking a prescribed medicine reluctantly, with no craving for the taste of meat, is not the same as breaking a vow out of desire. Please still ask the Bodhisattva's forgiveness sincerely and keep looking for a vegetarian replacement.

Alongside medical treatment, keep chanting the Great Compassion Mantra and pray: *"May the Greatly Merciful and Greatly Compassionate Guan Yin Bodhisattva bless me, [your full name], with good health and heal my spine and bones."* Buddhism supports medicine — it does not replace it.

Have you been doing daily recitation? If you tell me where you are with your practice, I can share what Master Lu taught for bone and spine conditions 🙏

*Reference: Buddhism Q&A (Answered — Frequently Asked Questions About Practising Buddhism)*

